 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158481 #edb/ref 1064 "Just like we can distribute multiplication over addition, we can also distribute conjunctions over disjunctions (and vice versa).\n\n- We can distribute a conjunction over a disjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\land (B \\lor C)  \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C)\n$$\n\n- Likewise, we can distribute a disjunction over a conjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\lor (B \\land C)  \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C)\n$$\n\nThe rules above are called the **distributive laws** for propositional logic. To prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables.\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\land (B \\lor C)  \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\land (B \\lor C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\lor (B \\land C)  \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor (B \\land C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158481 #edb/ref 1065 4343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158481 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d5455bd3-2038-5ab8-ac8b-2dd7ec1df636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158481 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1065 6591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1064 "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1065 6592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #ed